From: Sisyphus Aeolides <SisyphusAeolides@pm.me>
Date: Sun, 31 Aug 2026 16:29:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] mdadm: Gracefully handle missing sysfs module parameters

Some kernels do not expose every parameter that a given mdadm build may
attempt to write. The specific trigger is the legacy_async_del_gendisk
parameter introduced in 5.18 as a backward-compatibility knob: kernels
that pre-date that commit (e.g. 5.14.0-570.32.1.el9_6) never expose
the file at all, causing mdadm-4.4 to abort array assembly with:

  mdadm: Can't open /sys/module/md_mod/parameters/legacy_async_del_gendisk

The previous approach of calling access(path, F_OK) before open()
introduces a TOCTOU race (the sysfs node could disappear between the
two calls on module unload) and also conflates ENOENT with EACCES,
silently swallowing genuine permission errors.

Fix this by eliminating the access(2) pre-check entirely and instead
inspecting errno directly after the open(2) fails:

  - ENOENT: the parameter does not exist on this kernel; skip silently
    and return success, matching the "no-op is correct" intent.
  - Any other error (EACCES, EIO, ...): log the error with strerror()
    so the user has actionable diagnostic output, then return failure.

The same errno-aware error message is applied to the write(2) path so
that both failure modes produce informative output instead of bare
path strings.

Fixes: mdadm-4.4 regression on RHEL 9.6 / EL9 kernels < 5.18
Signed-off-by: Sisyphus Aeolides <SisyphusAeolides@pm.me>
---
 util.c | 16 ++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/util.c b/util.c
--- a/util.c
+++ b/util.c
@@ -2562,20 +2562,28 @@ bool set_md_mod_parameter(const char *name, const char *value)
 {
 	char path[PATH_MAX];
 	int fd;
-	int len;
+	ssize_t written;
+	int len;
+	int saved_errno;
 
 	snprintf(path, sizeof(path), "/sys/module/md_mod/parameters/%s", name);
 
 	fd = open(path, O_WRONLY);
 	if (fd < 0) {
-		pr_err("Can't open %s\n", path);
-		return false;
+		saved_errno = errno;
+		if (saved_errno == ENOENT) {
+			/*
+			 * This kernel does not expose the parameter at all.
+			 * That is not an error — the caller's intent is
+			 * already satisfied (the knob doesn't exist, so the
+			 * old behaviour is in effect by definition).
+			 */
+			return true;
+		}
+		pr_err("Can't open %s: %s\n", path, strerror(saved_errno));
+		return false;
 	}
 
 	len = strlen(value);
-	if (write(fd, value, len) != len) {
-		pr_err("Can't write to %s\n", path);
+	written = write(fd, value, len);
+	if (written != len) {
+		pr_err("Can't write to %s: %s\n", path,
+		       written < 0 ? strerror(errno) : "short write");
 		close(fd);
 		return false;
 	}
--
2.43.0
